      "text": "To solve the problem of finding how many different positive four-digit integers can be formed using the digits 2, 2, 9, and 9, we need to consider the permutations of these digits. Since the digits 2 and 9 are repeated, we use the formula for permutations of a multiset:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n!}{n_1! \\cdot n_2! \\cdot \\ldots \\cdot n_k!}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( n \\) is the total number of items to arrange, and \\( n_1, n_2, \\ldots, n_k \\) are the frequencies of the distinct items.\n\nIn this case, we have 4 digits in total (\\( n = 4 \\)), with 2 digits of 2 and 2 digits of 9 (\\( n_1 = 2 \\) and \\( n_2 = 2 \\)). Plugging these values into the formula, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{4!}{2! \\cdot 2!}\n\\]\n\nFirst, calculate the factorial values:\n\n\\[\n4! = 4 \\times 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 24\n\\]\n\\[\n2! = 2 \\times 1 = 2\n\\]\n\nNow, substitute these values back into the formula:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{24}{2 \\cdot 2} = \\frac{24}{4} = 6\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of different positive four-digit integers that can be formed using the digits 2, 2, 9, and 9 is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this by listing all the possible permutations:\n\n1. 2299\n2. 2929\n3. 2992\n4. 9229\n5. 9292\n6. 9922\n\nThere are indeed 6 different permutations, confirming our calculation. Therefore, the answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]",
