      "text": "To determine how many different positive four-digit integers can be formed using the digits 2, 2, 9, and 9, we need to consider the permutations of these digits, taking into account the repetition of the digits 2 and 9.\n\nFirst, we calculate the total number of permutations of the digits 2, 2, 9, and 9 without considering any repetitions. This is given by the formula for permutations of a multiset:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{4!}{2!2!}\n\\]\n\nHere, \\(4!\\) (4 factorial) is the total number of permutations of 4 digits, and \\(2!\\) (2 factorial) accounts for the repeated digits. So, we calculate it as follows:\n\n\\[\n4! = 4 \\times 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 24\n\\]\n\\[\n2! = 2 \\times 1 = 2\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{4!}{2!2!} = \\frac{24}{2 \\times 2} = \\frac{24}{4} = 6\n\\]\n\nTherefore, there are 6 different four-digit integers that can be formed using the digits 2, 2, 9, and 9. The list of these integers is:\n\n2299, 2929, 9229, 9292, 2992, 9922\n\nThus, the number of different positive four-digit integers that can be formed is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]",
