      "text": "To determine how many different positive four-digit integers can be formed using the digits 2, 2, 9, and 9, we need to count the distinct permutations of these digits. Since the digits 2 and 9 each appear twice, we can use the formula for permutations of a multiset.\n\nThe formula for the number of distinct permutations of a multiset is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n!}{n_1! \\cdot n_2! \\cdot \\ldots \\cdot n_k!}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( n \\) is the total number of items to arrange, and \\( n_1, n_2, \\ldots, n_k \\) are the frequencies of the distinct items.\n\nIn this problem, we have 4 digits in total (2, 2, 9, 9), so \\( n = 4 \\). The digit 2 appears 2 times, and the digit 9 appears 2 times. Therefore, the number of distinct permutations is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{4!}{2! \\cdot 2!}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we calculate \\( 4! \\) (4 factorial):\n\n\\[\n4! = 4 \\times 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 24\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate \\( 2! \\) (2 factorial):\n\n\\[\n2! = 2 \\times 1 = 2\n\\]\n\nNow, we substitute these values into the formula:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{4!}{2! \\cdot 2!} = \\frac{24}{2 \\cdot 2} = \\frac{24}{4} = 6\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of different positive four-digit integers that can be formed using the digits 2, 2, 9, and 9 is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).",
